# 013 — Multi-core support and hardware memory isolation — done

- Status: done
- Date: 2026-09-19

## Task statement

Run programs on all four QEMU CPUs. Move applications to ring 3,
give each instance its own CR3 and block access to the memory of the kernel and other
programs. Application faults must terminate only that instance.

## Acceptance criteria

- [x] The kernel starts the APs via INIT/SIPI; `cpus` shows the CPUs that are actually running.
- [x] Tasks are distributed across CPUs; `ps` shows each task's CPU.
- [x] Own GDT/TSS/IDT, separate kernel-entry stacks and emergency stacks.
- [x] Ring 3, IOPL=0, separate page tables; code RX, data/stack RW+NX.
- [x] Validation of all user pointers in syscalls, without reading kernel memory.
- [x] Page fault/GP/UD terminate only the offending task, with diagnostics.
- [x] Safe freeing after a task is stopped on another CPU.
- [x] `run`, `fg`, `kill`, Ctrl+Z, the clock and multiple instances work as before.
- [x] QEMU checks for SMP, isolation, faults, single-core mode and memory freeing.
- [x] Documentation and the built `usb_root/` updated.

## Implementation

- UEFI enumerates the processors via MP Services. Before ExitBootServices it reserves
  the ELF images, 64 MiB of runtime RAM, a separate 256 KiB BSP stack/BootInfo page below
  4 GiB and an AP bootstrap page below 1 MiB.
- The kernel starts the APs via INIT/SIPI. Up to 8 xAPIC CPUs are supported; in the standard QEMU
  setup all 4 run. The PIT on the BSP provides 100 Hz; LAPIC IPIs wake/preempt tasks on the APs.
- At launch a task is pinned to the CPU with the smallest number of live tasks.
  Round-robin runs on each CPU; user code executes in parallel.
  Shared scheduler and syscall data are protected by a spinlock that disables local IRQs.
- Per-CPU GDT/TSS, a shared IDT, separate entry stacks from ring 3,
  idle stacks, IST for double fault and NMI. The context saves GPRs and x87/SSE.
- A separate CR3 per process, identical user virtual addresses
  backed by different physical pages. The kernel identity map is supervisor-only.
  ELF code RX; mutable data, stack, mailbox and framebuffer RW+NX; BootInfo RO+NX.
  Page allocations do not share memory with neighbouring allocator objects; the stack is surrounded by
  unmapped guard pages. Relocations use the application's virtual base.
- Applications run with CPL=3 and IOPL=0. Only int 0x80 has DPL=3. All
  output pointers are validated against the calling process's page tables. The old firmware
  SYSCALL/SYSENTER entries are disabled, as are XSAVE/AVX and inherited global TLB entries.
- User exceptions terminate only the offending instance. `faults` keeps the last
  16 records with PID/CPU/vector/error/RIP/CR2. Kernel faults remain fatal.
- Before a process is freed, its CPU switches CR3 and stops considering the task
  current; the context is copied to a separate buffer, the entry stack belongs to the CPU.
  The BSP frees resources only after this point. With a fixed CPU and
  PCID/global user mappings disabled, a stale TLB does not survive a CR3 switch.
- `cpus` shows the CPUs that are actually online and counters; `ps` gained CPU/RUNNING.
  `run`, `fg`, `kill`, Ctrl+Z, `logs`, `heap` and the applications are preserved.
  Each core's idle uses HLT; `stop` sends a stop-IPI to the other CPUs.

## Verification

- Offline release build of the bootloader, kernel and three applications — no warnings.
- 17 host tests: ELF/relocations/RTC, scheduler policy, separate CR3s and physical
  pages, RX/RW+NX, guard pages, pointer errors and address overflow.
- QEMU, 4 CPUs: all 5 scenarios `normal`, `memory`, `busy`, `smp`, `isolation` passed.
  The earlier checks are kept: two copies of app/clock, independent colours/states,
  foreground, PS/2 keyboard and UART, task limit, repeated launches and OOM.
- SMP: eight infinite loops without WAIT, two on each CPU; CPU_TICKS increase
  on all of them, each loop keeps its own value in XMM0. A remote kill
  frees memory; after all tasks finish, HLT=1 was verified on every CPU.
- Isolation: the test ELF checks CPL=3/IOPL=0 itself. Reading/writing supervisor RAM,
  writing to RX code, executing the stack, CLI, OUT, UD2, a guard page, a corrupted RSP,
  SYSCALL/SYSENTER and invoking a privileged IDT vector cause an
  exception in that process only. The clock keeps running. The SYSENTER variant
  accounts for #UD on AMD64 or #GP on Intel with a zero SYSENTER_CS.
- Syscall rejections were checked for supervisor/noncanonical/overflow pointers and
  for a buffer crossing the boundary of an unmapped page. After the faults, memory returns
  exactly to its initial value, and new applications launch successfully.
- QEMU, 1 CPU: `smp` and `isolation` passed separately — 7 QEMU scenarios in total.
- `git diff --check` and the build script syntax check — no errors.
- `usb_root/` updated. The test ELFs run only in temporary FAT copies
  and do not replace the standard programs in the built package.

Logs of the current run: `/tmp/mind-core-<suite>-<N>cpu.log`.
The reproduction commands are given in the README.

## Stage boundaries

Target platform — UEFI x86-64/QEMU, xAPIC, NX, runtime RAM/GOP below 4 GiB.
Limits: 8 CPUs, 8 applications, 64 MiB runtime heap. No task migration, work stealing,
lazy page allocation or AVX contexts. The kernel identity map is supervisor-only,
but kernel text is not separately write-protected. Kernel/syscall work is serialized;
applications run in parallel. Configurations with 1 and 4 CPUs were verified.
